@DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress = http://localhost:5189
@meetingId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@participantId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001
@topicId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002
@proposalId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003

### Create a meeting
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"title": "Projection Meeting: Architecture review",
	"description": "Review the next Meeting use cases.",
	"startsAt": "2026-09-24T10:00:00Z",
	"endsAt": "2026-09-24T11:00:00Z"
}

### Add a participant
# Replace @meetingId with the id returned by the create-meeting request.
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}/participants
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"name": "Alex Morgan",
	"email": "alex.morgan@example.com"
}

### Get meeting (copy the participant ID and General topic ID from the response)
GET {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}

### Create another topic
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}/topics
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"title": "Pricing"
}

### Create a proposal under the selected topic
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}/topics/{{topicId}}/proposals
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"title": "Increase by 7%",
	"description": "Apply a 7% price increase next quarter."
}

### Add a topic-level discussion entry (omit proposalId)
# Replace @participantId and @topicId with IDs from the meeting response.
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}/topics/{{topicId}}/discussion-entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"authorParticipantId": "{{participantId}}",
	"content": "Let's review the expected customer impact."
}

### Add a proposal-level discussion entry
# Replace @proposalId with the proposal ID returned by the meeting response.
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}/topics/{{topicId}}/discussion-entries?proposalId={{proposalId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"authorParticipantId": "{{participantId}}",
	"content": "This proposal may be easier to phase in."
}

### Get all discussion entries for the meeting
GET {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}/discussion-entries

### Filter discussion entries by topic
GET {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}/discussion-entries?topicId={{topicId}}

### Filter discussion entries by proposal
GET {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{meetingId}}/discussion-entries?proposalId={{proposalId}}

### End-to-end smoke test: create a meeting through generated minutes
# Prerequisites:
# - Start PostgreSQL and KurrentDB, apply migrations, and run the .NET API.
# - Start the Python minutes worker before sending the Complete request below.
# - Configure GEMINI_API_KEY for the worker and the same service key for
#   MINUTES_SERVICE_API_KEY (worker) and MinutesService__ApiKey (.NET API).
# - See docs/local-development.md for startup, migration, and worker commands.
# Run these requests in order. VS Code REST Client captures IDs from earlier
# responses. The PostgreSQL discussion projection is asynchronous; repeat the
# projection GET until the discussion entry appears.

### 1. Create a meeting
# @name smokeCreateMeeting
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"title": "Minutes pipeline smoke test",
	"description": "End-to-end verification of KurrentDB, PostgreSQL projection, and Python minutes generation.",
	"startsAt": "2030-01-15T10:00:00Z",
	"endsAt": "2030-01-15T10:30:00Z"
}

@smokeMeetingId = {{smokeCreateMeeting.response.body.$.id}}

### 2. Add a participant
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{smokeMeetingId}}/participants
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"name": "Minutes Pipeline Tester",
	"email": "minutes-pipeline-tester@example.com"
}

### 3. Read the meeting to capture its participant and General topic IDs
# @name smokeGetMeetingIds
GET {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{smokeMeetingId}}

@smokeParticipantId = {{smokeGetMeetingIds.response.body.$.participants[0].id}}
@smokeGeneralTopicId = {{smokeGetMeetingIds.response.body.$.topics[0].id}}

### 4. Start the meeting
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{smokeMeetingId}}/start

### 5. Add a discussion entry to the General topic
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{smokeMeetingId}}/topics/{{smokeGeneralTopicId}}/discussion-entries
Content-Type: application/json

{
	"authorParticipantId": "{{smokeParticipantId}}",
	"content": "We agreed to prepare a concise summary of the discussion and next steps."
}

### 6. Verify the discussion entry reached the PostgreSQL projection
# Repeat this request if the projection has not caught up yet.
GET {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{smokeMeetingId}}/discussion-entries

### 7. Complete the meeting to trigger minutes generation
# The Python worker must already be running; this emits meeting.completed.v1.
POST {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{smokeMeetingId}}/complete

### 8. Check for the generated minutes draft
# Repeat this GET while the worker processes the event. Success is a non-empty
# minutesDrafts array in the meeting response.
GET {{DecisionPlannerApi_HostAddress}}/api/meetings/{{smokeMeetingId}}
